    "id": "prop:fixed-graph-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If \\(\\mathfrak G_{H,L}(\\mathcal S)=\\{G_0\\}\\), then the displayed refinement envelope equals the extrema of the single raw canonical polynomial program for \\(G_0\\). If, additionally, \\(E_{G_0}^{\\leftrightarrow}=\\varnothing\\), the program reduces to independent node-response factors for the known DAG.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\mathfrak G_{H,L}(\\mathcal S)",
      "\\mathcal S",
      "H",
      "L",
      "G",
      "E_G^{\\leftrightarrow}",
      "\\underline q(p)",
      "\\overline q(p)",
      "p"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:structural-recursion",
      "def:raw-polynomial-compiler",
      "thm:sharp-refinement-envelope"
    ],
    "justification": "The new union must collapse to the established fixed-graph response-function analysis when lag uncertainty vanishes.",
    "gap": "This is a fidelity reduction to DuarteEtAl2024 rather than a separate novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "Existing fixed-graph Autobounds workflows become a special case and provide regression tests for the refinement compiler.",
    "proof_tex": "If \\(\\mathfrak G_{H,L}(\\mathcal S)=\\{G_0\\}\\), the outer minimum and maximum in \\(\\text{thm:sharp-refinement-envelope}\\) range over a singleton.  Therefore\n\\[\n[\\underline q(p),\\overline q(p)]\n=\\left[\n\\min_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_{G_0}:P_{G_0}(\\lambda)=p}q_{G_0}(\\lambda),\n\\max_{\\lambda\\in\\Lambda_{G_0}:P_{G_0}(\\lambda)=p}q_{G_0}(\\lambda)\n\\right],\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the single raw program in \\(\\text{def:raw-polynomial-compiler}\\).  If \\(E_{G_0}^{\\leftrightarrow}=\\varnothing\\), the exogenous indices \\(a\\) are only the singletons \\(\\{v\\}\\).  The independence assumption then makes the joint response mass\n\\[\n\\prod_{v\\in\\mathcal W_H}\\lambda_{\\{v\\}}(u_{\\{v\\}}),\n\\]\nand each \\(f_v\\) uses its observed DAG parents and its private response state only.  Hence the program is exactly the product of independent node-response factors for the known DAG.",
